# P18-V01 offset-boundary decode and current-revision release gates — 2026-10-17T20:51:44Z

- Repository: `/Users/bruce.tsai/Projects/trio/rocketmq-classic`.
- Branch: `phase/19-phase-18-compatibility-release-gate`.
- Code revision: `9513e4da fix(protocol): match offset boundary header reflection`.
- Java oracle: RocketMQ client 5.5.1, source commit
  `efa1be59d6fc747ad220f036c86030f05f0419f9`, inspected read-only.

## Parity change

- Java's `SearchOffsetResponseHeader`, `GetMaxOffsetResponseHeader`,
  `GetMinOffsetResponseHeader`, and `GetEarliestMsgStoretimeResponseHeader`
  use generic reflection even when the fast-encode hint is set. A missing or
  malformed boxed `Long` conversion is caught per field and leaves that value
  null. Each Java RPC owner later fails while unboxing the required value.
- Rust now preserves these fields as `Option<i64>` in direct mode-aware header
  decoding and validates them at the corresponding RPC owner boundary.
- The invoked Java fixture covers absent fields, malformed numeric input,
  valid values, both mode hints, and owner behavior. Fixture SHA-256:
  `c4891f046639eb0f9a82e221b1605d296aed1314fb70e37c22ea514d240a9e95`.
- This closes only the four offset-boundary header/owner slices. Broad P18-V01
  source/API/settings/input/error/flow and reverse-export reconciliation stays
  open.

## P18-V02 server-free verification

The complete gate passed on code revision `9513e4da` in a serialized Linux
ARM64 `rust:1.85.0-bookworm` Docker environment:

- Default and all-feature workspace tests passed. All-target/all-feature
  workspace check and strict Clippy passed.
- Maven `verify` passed all 37 scenarios, fixture-drift checks, and Java/Rust
  cross-decodes.
- Differential comparator self-test and 37-row manifest validation passed.
- Offline race/leak smoke passed 16 cases × 3 repetitions. Fuzz smoke passed
  105 frame seeds, 11 message seeds, 1,856 mutations, 146 frame decodes, and
  113 message decodes.
- Release examples and workspace benchmark targets compiled. The offline
  benchmark ran on Linux ARM64 with Rust 1.85.0; measured per-iteration
  observations were 1,192.9 ns for frame encode/decode, 704.3 ns for Zstd
  message decode, 782.5 ns for Producer request construction, 11.9 ns for
  128-entry route lookup, and 5,372.8 ns for pending request completion. These
  are observations, not thresholds.
- Locked Cargo metadata/package listings, workspace metadata/license/dependency
  checks, and seven package listings containing `LICENSE` and `NOTICE` passed.
  Four Core Unique ID tests passed under both UTC and Asia/Taipei. Formatting
  and diff checks passed.
- An initial parallel workspace run had one Consumer stack-capture test fail
  because the environment exposed no unblocked signal. The serialized default
  and all-feature reruns passed.

